{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"title","type":"text","text":"# The Italian Campaign 1796-97\n\nFull campaign storyboard\nPlayer: Private in the 14th Demi-Brigade\n\n13 chapters + epilogue\nRivoli demo is Chapter 11","x":0,"y":0,"width":440,"height":200,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"ch1","type":"text","text":"# Ch 1 — Army of Italy\n*March 1796 · Nice*\n\nNapoleon arrives — 26 years old. Army is starving, unpaid, some without shoes or muskets. Senior officers (Massena, Augereau, Serurier) see him as a political appointment.\n\nPlayer enlists / is already serving. Establish character, companions, daily misery. Berthier reorganises supply; the army prepares to move.\n\n**Themes:** Hunger, discipline, rumours of new general\n**Key NPCs:** Pierre, Jean-Baptiste, Sergeant\n**Tone:** Grim, restless anticipation","x":0,"y":280,"width":440,"height":340,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"ch2","type":"text","text":"# Ch 2 — Montenotte\n*12-15 April 1796*\n\nFirst real combat. 38,000 French vs separated Austrian/Piedmontese. Night marches in rain and fog across steep ravines. Three victories in four days.\n\n**Battles:** Montenotte, Cosseria, Dego\n**Key moments:**\n- Beaulieu (70, Austrian commander) strikes first at Voltri — plays into Napoleon's hands\n- Colonel Rampon: \"Here, we must conquer or die\"\n- Cosseria: brutal frontal assaults, hundreds of casualties. Colonel of 18th killed; Suchet (26) takes command. Marmont: \"I have never seen fire like it\"\n- Dego: hungry troops plunder, Colonel Vukassovich (Croatian) retakes it — position lost, must be refought\n- Lannes' bayonet charge at Dego wins particular praise from Napoleon\n\n**Themes:** Baptism of fire, exhaustion, first taste of plunder","x":0,"y":700,"width":440,"height":500,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ch3","type":"text","text":"# Ch 3 — Mondovi & Piedmont\n*April 1796*\n\nPursuit of General Colli's Piedmontese. Soldiers loot freely — first moral crisis.\n\n**Key moments:**\n- Crossing swollen Corsaglia under fire\n- Serurier's troops loot San Michele, thrown out by counterattack\n- Napoleon detects Colli's fake campfires, launches exhausted troops in pursuit\n- General Dichat killed; retreat turns to rout. French enter Mondovi — at last, they can eat and rest\n- Descend into fertile plain — starving troops plunder countryside\n- Joubert: \"Not a day passes without looters being shot\"\n- Massena and Augereau among the worst offenders\n- Armistice: Piedmont cedes Cuneo, Alessandria, Nice, Savoy. War over in 3 weeks.\n\n**Themes:** Discipline vs survival, moral choices about looting","x":0,"y":1280,"width":440,"height":480,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ch4","type":"text","text":"# Ch 4 — Crossing the Po & Lodi\n*May 1796*\n\n40 miles in 36 hours to cross the Po. Then the bridge at Lodi — 200 yards swept by 14 guns.\n\n**Key moments:**\n- 7,000 reinforcements arrive; elite brigade formed under Dallemagne\n- Lannes leads the Po crossing at Piacenza; Massena feints at Sale\n- Battle of Fombio (8 May): confused night fighting — General La Harpe killed, possibly by friendly fire. Napoleon regards him as one of the army's best.\n- Lodi (10 May): artillery duel across the Adda\n- 2nd Carabiniers lead the charge — \"Vive la Republique!\"\n- Napoleon, Berthier, Massena, Lannes urging men forward through torrential fire\n- Cavalry sent upstream to find ford, threatening Austrian rear\n- Soldiers nickname Napoleon \"le petit caporal\" — he aims guns himself, does a corporal's job\n- In just a month: war-weary army transformed into a victorious fighting force brimming with esprit de corps\n\n**Themes:** Esprit de corps born, personal courage, the legend begins","x":0,"y":1840,"width":440,"height":540,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ch5","type":"text","text":"# Ch 5 — Milan & Lombardy\n*May-June 1796*\n\nOccupation. Cheering crowds, then revolt. The ugly side of liberation.\n\n**Key moments:**\n- Triumphal entry into Milan (15 May)\n- French demand 20M francs, requisition food, horses, cattle, boots\n- Crossing the Mincio at Borghetto — Austrian counterattack nearly captures Napoleon (\"hopping over fences with one boot on\"). Formation of the Guides a Cheval under Bessieres — Napoleon's personal escort.\n- Attacks on churches enrage Italian priests and peasants\n- Revolt in Pavia (23 May) — Lannes' grenadiers burn Binasco. Napoleon: \"A terrible example, which will be effective.\"\n- Ringleaders (including priests) shot, hundreds of hostages taken\n- Army reorganised: four divisions (Kilmaine, Augereau, Massena, Serurier)\n\n**Themes:** Occupation duty, civilian interactions, propaganda vs reality\n**Tone:** Uneasy peace, garrison life, moral ambiguity","x":0,"y":2460,"width":440,"height":520,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"ch6","type":"text","text":"# Ch 6 — First Siege of Mantua\n*June-July 1796*\n\nThe army grinds to a halt. Malarial swamps. Endless waiting.\nSince April: three offensives, 200+ miles marched, ten battles won — now brought to a halt.\n\n**Key moments:**\n- \"The most unhealthy place in Italy\" — Napoleon\n- Siege without heavy guns (intercepted at sea by Commodore Nelson)\n- Troops sicken in summer marshes; sick pile up at Brescia hospital\n- Must simultaneously: maintain siege, protect communications, guard against revolt, face reinforcements\n- Waiting, waiting, then Wurmser's relief columns descend\n\n**Themes:** Boredom, disease, the siege as ordeal\n**Tone:** Oppressive, stagnant","x":0,"y":3060,"width":440,"height":400,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"ch7","type":"text","text":"# Ch 7 — Castiglione\n*July-August 1796*\n\nNapoleon's greatest crisis so far. Outnumbered, left flank shattered, possible encirclement. He spots that Lake Garda separates the Austrian columns — defeat each in turn.\n\n**Battles:** Lonato, Castiglione\n**Key moments:**\n- Abandon siege — 179 guns dumped in marshes\n- Brescia lost — army hospital with 2,000 sick captured. Josephine nearly taken.\n- Napoleon's Lonato bluff: 3 lost Austrian battalions demand his surrender. He loudly orders nonexistent troops into position. Austrians meekly lay down arms.\n- Junot wounded six times at Lonato, accepts Austrian commander's surrender\n- Napoleon to the 32nd: \"Bayonets only — and you, 32nd, maintain your glorious reputation.\"\n- Augereau's heroic rearguard — division at breaking point: \"If you don't send me reinforcements, I won't be able to hold out.\"\n- Castiglione (5 Aug): feigned retreat lures Wurmser forward. Fiorella arrives behind Austrian left. Marmont's battery on Monte Medolano. 4th and 5th Demi-Brigades storm Solferino heights.\n- Wurmser in shock: \"watched without seeing anything, saying anything, hearing anything. This brave old man... did not think anymore.\"\n- Week of forced marches in stifling heat, little food\n\n**Themes:** Crisis and comeback, physical limits, the bluff","x":0,"y":3540,"width":440,"height":680,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ch8","type":"text","text":"# Ch 8 — Bassano & the Brenta\n*September 1796*\n\nDirectory orders Napoleon to break through to Innsbruck and join Moreau. But Wurmser has his own plan — wide flanking march via the Brenta to relieve Mantua from the east.\n\n**Battles:** Rovereto, Primolano, Bassano\n**Key moments:**\n- Serurier contracts malaria, returns to France. Even Massena asks to be replaced — Napoleon refuses.\n- General Victor leads main attack at Rovereto; light infantry flanking in mountain passes\n- Rovereto: Austrians preparing dinner when French breakthrough. 3,000 prisoners, 25 guns, 7 standards.\n- Napoleon tears up the Moreau plan, orders pursuit down the Brenta\n- Lannes leads charge at Bassano; Murat's cavalry chases Austrians through town. 2,000 more prisoners, 30 guns.\n- Footrace to Mantua — for once, Austrians outmarch the exhausted French\n- Rare defeat for Massena at Cerea (Ott clears the road — 700 French prisoners)\n- Wurmser reaches Mantua with just 12,000 men — saved by the skin of his teeth\n\n**Themes:** Momentum, mountain warfare, overextension","x":0,"y":4300,"width":440,"height":560,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"ch9","type":"text","text":"# Ch 9 — Caldiero\n*October-November 1796*\n\nThe war's lowest ebb. A new Austrian commander, a new army, and Napoleon's first defeat.\n\n**Key moments:**\n- Siege of Mantua restarts from scratch. ~100 Austrian soldiers dying per day inside from disease.\n- Alvinczi (61, Hungarian) arrives with 26,000 + Davidovich's 18,000 + Wurmser's 12,000 in Mantua\n- Second Battle of Bassano (6 Nov): bloodiest day of the campaign. ~3,000 casualties each side. A failure for Napoleon.\n- Disaster at Calliano: Vaubois loses nearly half his division\n- Battle of Caldiero (12 Nov): 32nd Demi-Brigade reaches the ridge, but French outflanked on both wings. Napoleon retreats to Verona. His first unequivocal defeat.\n- Napoleon's letter: \"The Army of Italy, reduced to a handful of men, is worn out... The heroes of Lodi, Millesimo, Castiglione and Bassano have died for their country or are in the hospitals.\"\n\n**Themes:** Rock bottom before Arcole, the legend falters, what defeat feels like\n**Tone:** Despair, exhaustion, defiance","x":0,"y":4940,"width":440,"height":520,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"ch10","type":"text","text":"# Ch 10 — Arcole\n*15-17 November 1796*\n\nThree days in the marshes. Napoleon's darkest hour.\n\n**Day 1:**\n- Overnight march; cross Adige on pontoon. Marshland — stuck on narrow causeway, 20 yards wide.\n- Colonel Lannes — discharged himself from hospital that morning — leads charge, hit in leg.\n- Augereau grabs standard: \"Grenadiers! Come and seek your colour!\" — nobody follows.\n- Napoleon charges bridge, Colonel Muiron killed beside him.\n- Sulkowski: \"I was witness to this extraordinary cowardice, and I cannot conceive it.\"\n- Napoleon's horse loses footing — tumbles into swamp, hauled out by aides.\n\n**Day 2:**\n- Stalemate. Massena's skirmishers in marshland; Austrians bunched on causeway.\n- Augereau still can't get close. Soldiers camp amid marshes.\n\n**Day 3:**\n- 51st Demi-Brigade — only unit across broken pontoon — advances gamely alone.\n- Bloody see-saw. Brigadier General Robert killed; Augereau's men panic.\n- Massena sends in the 32nd — sudden counter-attack turns the tide.\n- Napoleon's ruse: 25 horsemen blowing bugles to feign mass cavalry charge.\n\nThree-day battle: messy, bloody, narrow margin. A third of the army are casualties. But the legend is born — sensational PR captivates France.\n\n**Themes:** Courage and cowardice, the bridge as crucible","x":0,"y":5540,"width":440,"height":700,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"ch11","type":"text","text":"# ★ Ch 11 — RIVOLI ★\n*14 January 1797*\n\n**THIS IS THE CURRENT DEMO**\n→ [[Game Scope 1/Rivoli Demo/Storyboard|Rivoli Demo Storyboard]]\n\nSurrounded. Outnumbered 3:1. Six Austrian columns — five visible by campfire at midnight (Lusignan's lost on Monte Baldo in deep snow).\n\nNapoleon arrives at midnight, inspects positions with Joubert (27, player's divisional commander): \"The moonlight was superb... the atmosphere was ablaze with [enemy campfires].\"\n\n**14th Demi-Brigade featured:**\n- 85th routed, 29th Light retreats — the 14th holds\n- \"Will you let them take your guns?\"\n- Ferocious charge reclaims battery\n- Massena arrives at 9am, stabilises the line\n- Leclerc and Lasalle's cavalry charge into the gorge\n- Ammunition wagon explodes — Austrian morale breaks\n- Mass rout by 1pm. Lusignan destroyed by Rey.\n\n**Followed by:** Race south, 57th charges Provera's flank at La Favorita, Wurmser forced back into Citadel. Provera surrenders.","x":0,"y":6320,"width":440,"height":560,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"ch12","type":"text","text":"# Ch 12 — Fall of Mantua\n*2 February 1797*\n\nThe siege ends. Eight months. Wurmser surrenders 16,000 survivors.\n\n**Key moments:**\n- Two more agonising weeks after Rivoli until all food gone (rations ran out 27 Jan)\n- ~100 Austrian soldiers had been dying per day for six weeks\n- 9,000 dead from disease/wounds/malnutrition in 4 months\n- Civilians eating rats and dogs; survivors on horsemeat\n- Wurmser and escort allowed to return to Austria with honours — the conventions of 18th-century war\n- Serurier (not Napoleon — already gone) takes formal surrender\n- 44,000 total Austrian losses across the campaign\n\n**Themes:** The cost of siege, compassion for the enemy, what victory looks like up close","x":0,"y":6960,"width":440,"height":420,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"ch13","type":"text","text":"# Ch 13 — March on Vienna\n*March-April 1797*\n\n70,000 confident, seasoned troops. Three-pronged advance.\n\n**Key moments:**\n- Napoleon marches south first — Victor sweeps aside Papal forces at Faenza\n- Treaty of Tolentino: Pope gives up Romagna, 30M francs, 100 works of art\n- Joubert invades the Tyrol; Massena up the Piave valley; Napoleon leads the main body\n- Archduke Charles (25, Emperor's brother) takes command — defeated Jourdan and Moreau on the Rhine. A real threat, but not enough troops or time.\n- Bernadotte surrounds 2,000 Austrians at Tagliamento\n- Massena blocks retreat at Tarvis — 3,000 more prisoners in three days\n- Supply lines overextended — Napoleon proposes peace while advancing\n- Armistice\n\n**Themes:** Veteran confidence, overreach, war-weariness","x":0,"y":7460,"width":440,"height":440,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"epilogue","type":"text","text":"# Epilogue — Campo Formio\n*October 1797*\n\nThe treaty. What did it all mean?\n\n**For the soldier:** A year of marching, fighting, starving, plundering. Comrades dead. Italy transformed.\n**For France:** Austrian Netherlands (Belgium) gained. Venice — a 1,200-year republic — destroyed, divided between France and Austria. Its Horses of St. Mark sent to the Louvre.\n**For Napoleon:** From unknown 26-year-old to the most famous man in Europe. Authors the Cisalpine Republic's constitution himself.\n\nThe legend is made. Egypt awaits.","x":0,"y":7980,"width":440,"height":320,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"thread-plunder","type":"text","text":"## Recurring Thread: Plunder & Discipline\n\nThree layers run through the campaign:\n\n**1. Soldier looting (hunger-driven):**\n- Dego: troops plunder, position lost and must be refought\n- San Michele: Serurier's troops loot, thrown out by counterattack\n\n**2. Officer corruption:**\n- Massena & Augereau among the worst offenders\n- Joubert: \"Not a day passes without looters being shot\"\n- Looters executed daily — the mania doesn't stop\n\n**3. State extraction:**\n- Milan: 20M francs demanded, everything requisitioned\n- Tolentino: 30M francs, 100 artworks from the Pope\n- Horses of St. Mark shipped to Paris\n\nPlayer moral choices throughout all three layers.","x":580,"y":700,"width":360,"height":480,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"thread-exhaustion","type":"text","text":"## Recurring Thread: Exhaustion\n\n- Start: starving, shoeless, some without muskets\n- Montenotte: 3 battles in 4 days\n- Po crossing: 40 miles in 36 hours (elite brigade)\n- Castiglione: week of forced marches in stifling heat\n- Even Massena asks to be replaced (Sept) — Napoleon refuses\n- Bassano: for once, Austrians outmarch the exhausted French\n- Caldiero: \"The Army of Italy, reduced to a handful of men, is worn out\"\n- Arcole: 3 days in marshes, a third of army casualties\n- Rivoli: unpaid for weeks, uniforms disintegrating, shoes broken","x":580,"y":1260,"width":360,"height":340,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"thread-legend","type":"text","text":"## Recurring Thread: The Legend\n\nNapoleon's image grows — but not in a straight line:\n\n- Nice: \"puny face\" — grows 2 feet with his hat\n- Lodi: \"le petit caporal\" born — does a corporal's job\n- Lonato: bluffs 3 Austrian battalions into surrendering\n- **Caldiero: first defeat — the legend falters**\n- Arcole: charges bridge, tumbles in swamp → sensational PR captivates France\n- Rivoli: calm amid encirclement, midnight reconnaissance by moonlight\n- Marmont carries captured standards to Paris — the legend reaches the capital\n- Campo Formio: most famous man in Europe\n\nPlayer witnesses this transformation firsthand","x":580,"y":1680,"width":360,"height":400,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"thread-14th","type":"text","text":"## The 14th Demi-Brigade\n\nPlayer's unit throughout the campaign.\n\n**At Rivoli (confirmed):** Joubert's division.\n- Intense fighting in vineyards & walled gardens\n- Other units (85th, 29th Light) break — the 14th holds\n- Reclaimed overrun battery\n\n**Earlier campaign:** Creative license. The 14th is only named at Rivoli in the source. Joubert's division was northern — not present at Lodi, Castiglione, or Arcole.\n\n**Options:**\n- Player transfers between divisions\n- Player hears of other battles secondhand\n- Embrace creative license for narrative","x":580,"y":2160,"width":360,"height":380,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"thread-disease","type":"text","text":"## Recurring Thread: Disease & Medical Care\n\nThe invisible enemy — kills more than combat:\n\n- Mantua: malarial swamps, \"most unhealthy place in Italy\"\n- Brescia: hospital with 2,000 sick captured by Austrians\n- Serurier contracts malaria, evacuated to France\n- Second siege: ~100 Austrians dying per day for 6 weeks\n- \"The hospitals lack everything, our wounded are lying on the floors\"\n- 9,000 Austrian garrison dead from disease in 4 months\n- \"Medical care affects morale — troops want to know they'll be looked after\"\n\nDirectly relevant to player health/morale systems.","x":580,"y":2620,"width":360,"height":380,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"thread-austrian","type":"text","text":"## Recurring Thread: Austrian Commanders\n\nA new general, a new army, a new plan — each time:\n\n1. **Beaulieu** (70) — doesn't know Italy, fixated on Genoa. Defeated Apr-May.\n2. **Wurmser** (72) — courageous cavalryman. Two relief attempts. Trapped in Mantua, surrenders Feb 1797.\n3. **Alvinczi** (61) — Hungarian. Wins at Caldiero (Napoleon's first defeat). Loses at Arcole and Rivoli.\n4. **Archduke Charles** (25) — Emperor's brother, beat Jourdan & Moreau. Real talent, but too late, too few.\n\nSource of dark humour, dread, and weary resignation for the common soldier. The war keeps going.","x":580,"y":3080,"width":360,"height":400,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"thread-mantua","type":"text","text":"## Recurring Thread: The Siege of Mantua\n\nThe strategic fulcrum — every battle relates to it:\n\n- Ch 6: First siege begins (June). No heavy guns.\n- Ch 7: Abandoned — 179 guns dumped. Wurmser breaks through.\n- Ch 7: Castiglione victory. Wurmser retreats.\n- Ch 8: Wurmser marches down Brenta to relieve. Fails, trapped inside.\n- Ch 9: Second siege restarts. 100 dying per day.\n- Ch 10: Arcole — Alvinczi's relief attempt fails.\n- Ch 11: Rivoli — Provera's last attempt fails.\n- Ch 12: Falls at last. 8 months. 9,000 Austrian dead.\n\nThe war's Sisyphean quality: keep winning, siege grinds on.","x":580,"y":3560,"width":360,"height":400,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"thread-officers","type":"text","text":"## Recurring Thread: Officers at the Front\n\nRank is no protection in the Army of Italy.\n\n**Killed:** La Harpe (friendly fire), Muiron (beside Napoleon at Arcole), Robert (Arcole Day 3), Dichat (Mondovi pursuit), Colonel of the 18th (Cosseria)\n\n**Wounded repeatedly:** Lannes (bayonet charges, hospital discharge), Junot (6 times at Lonato)\n\n**Leading charges:** Napoleon (Lodi, Arcole), Augereau (grabs standard at Arcole), Rampon (\"conquer or die\"), Suchet (26, takes command when colonel killed)\n\nFrom a private's view: admiration, fear, the knowledge that your officers fight and die beside you. Feeds the Legend.","x":580,"y":4040,"width":360,"height":420,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"thread-cavalry","type":"text","text":"## Recurring Thread: Cavalry as External Force\n\nPlayer is infantry — cavalry appears at turning points:\n\n- Stengel leads cavalry at campaign start\n- Lodi: cavalry sent upstream to find ford, threatening Austrian rear\n- Borghetto: Austrian cavalry nearly captures Napoleon → Guides formed\n- Bassano: Murat's cavalry chases Austrians through town\n- Arcole: 25 horsemen blowing bugles — the bluff that breaks Arcole\n- Rivoli: Leclerc & Lasalle charge into the gorge — the moment the battle turns\n\nThunder and hooves from the infantry perspective.","x":580,"y":4540,"width":360,"height":340,"color":"1"}
	],
